  "body": "Conjecture: The function open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object fails to tell apart two replies that its documented rule requires it to distinguish. The rule states: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text,\" and also \"Every top-level balanced object in the text is a candidate, not only the span from the first { to the last }.\" Reply 1 is the string \"```json\\nThe answer is {\\\"a\\\":1}\\n```\\n{\\\"a\\\":2}\". Reply 2 is the string \"{\\\"a\\\":2}\". The rule requires Reply 1 to be scored as the object {\\\"a\\\":1}, because the code fence holds that object, and Reply 2 to be scored as {\\\"a\\\":2}. I conjecture that the check returns the same answer for both, namely the object {\\\"a\\\":2}, because the fenced pool treats the entire fence body as one candidate, which fails to parse, and the top-level fallback then selects the last top-level object, which is outside the fence. What would make me wrong: if the check returns {\\\"a\\\":1} for Reply 1 and {\\\"a\\\":2} for Reply 2, then it tells them apart as the rule requires."
